---
<Page title="Evidence overview: GHES 3.22, chaos and latency tests | Graftyard"
  description="The tests behind Graftyard: real GitHub clients against GHES 3.22 schemas, 104.6 hours of chaos on Cloudflare, token revocation over HTTPS, and measured commit latency."
  current="evidence" doc>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="ev-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="doc-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Evidence overview</p>
        <h1 id="ev-h">What we tested, and what it showed</h1>
        <p class="t-lead">Graftyard ran on Cloudflare in test installs across North America, Europe and Asia-Pacific. Real clients hit it, a chaos driver attacked it, and checkers read back every site's log. These are the results through 24 November 2026, failures included.</p>
        <p class="more"><a class="link" href="/evidence/all/">Every check, benchmark and baseline, in full<Fragment set:html={ARROW} /></a></p>
      </div>
      <ul class="kpis">
        <li><b>348 of 348</b><span>git client checks on three git versions, a site failing in half</span></li>
        <li><b>0 of 3,588,424</b><span>acknowledged writes lost in audited chaos runs</span></li>
        <li><b>104.6 h</b><span>of chaos on Cloudflare, 12,304,207 operations checked</span></li>
        <li><b>172 to 370 ms</b><span>median commit across two continents</span></li>
        <li><b>200 of 200</b><span>revoked tokens refused on the next push, over HTTPS</span></li>
        <li><b>759</b><span>Rust tests in the workspace, 0 failing</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="ghes" aria-labelledby="ghes-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="span-5">
          <p class="eyebrow">GHES 3.22 compatibility</p>
          <h2 id="ghes-h" style="margin-top: var(--s-3)">Unmodified clients, checked against the GHES schemas</h2>
          <p class="t-lead" style="margin-top: var(--s-5)">No client was patched and no request went to github.com. The host answers <code>/api/v3/meta</code> with <code>installed_version</code> 3.22.0, so gh, git-lfs, VS Code and JetBrains all take their GHES path.</p>
        </div>
        <Film name="ghes-loop" label="gh and git against a Graftyard host: the host reports GHES 3.22.0 and each client check passes" class="span-7 start-6" />
      </div>
      <h3 style="margin-top: var(--s-9)">Clients</h3>
      <Table head={['Client and commands', 'Where', 'Result']} rows={clientRows} />
      <h3 style="margin-top: var(--s-8)">Schemas</h3>
      <Table head={['Check', 'Scope', 'Result']} rows={schemaRows} />
      <p class="note">The GraphQL schema is generated from the GHES 3.22 SDL (1,626 types). <code>gh pr checks</code> exits 1 on a pull request with no checks, which is gh's own answer for that case; every request in it returned 200. Terraform runs live through the provider's own acceptance test. VS Code, JetBrains, Jenkins and Argo CD are covered by reading their source for the GHES paths they call, not yet by a live run.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="chaos" aria-labelledby="chaos-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="span-5">
          <p class="eyebrow">Chaos and linearizability</p>
          <h2 id="chaos-h" style="margin-top: var(--s-3)">Seven runs with faults on, 104.6 hours</h2>
          <p class="t-lead" style="margin-top: var(--s-5)">Every 8 to 16 s the driver reset a member or moved leadership. 5% of calls between sites failed and 5% of replication replies were dropped. Clients in Atlanta, London and Singapore kept writing.</p>
          <ul class="facts">
            <li><b>Porcupine</b> checked each history for linearizability, key by key.</li>
            <li><b>Lost-write audit:</b> after a run, all three sites' stored logs are read back and every acknowledged write is looked up.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <Film name="chaos-loop" label="Members reset and leadership moves between three sites while writes keep landing and none is lost" class="span-7 start-6" />
      </div>
      <h3 style="margin-top: var(--s-9)">The runs on Cloudflare</h3>
      <Table head={['Run', 'Length', 'Operations', 'Faults injected', 'Result']} rows={chaosRows} />
      <p class="note">Recorded-clock checks flag some cross-site reads as Illegal; with 100 ms of clock allowance, or with the measured bound, every passing run is Ok on all keys. <code>lin24b</code>'s 20 flagged segments trace to 15 lost client log chunks and 2 ties needing 1 and 4 ms of skew, against 63 to 92 ms measured that minute.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: var(--s-8)">Targeted fault gates</h3>
      <Table head={['Fault', 'Runs', 'Result']} rows={gateRows} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="events" aria-labelledby="events-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Webhooks, notifications and search</p>
        <h2 id="events-h">Every event comes from a committed change, and none is lost</h2>
        <p>Webhooks, notifications and search are fed from each repository's committed log through cursors. An event exists only if its change committed, and a crash means a resend, never a gap.</p>
      </div>
      <Table head={['Check', 'Runs', 'Result']} rows={eventRows} />
      <p class="note">On the hosted test install <code>graft-m4a</code>, 13 and 14 November 2026. Each repeat delivery keeps the GUID of its first attempt, so receivers that dedupe by <code>X-GitHub-Delivery</code> see each event once.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="actions" aria-labelledby="actions-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Actions</p>
        <h2 id="actions-h">Unmodified GitHub runners, with sites failing under them</h2>
        <p>Jobs run on the stock <code>actions/runner</code>, the same binary GitHub ships. Each check kills something mid-run and then reads back what every job and check ended as.</p>
      </div>
      <Table head={['Check', 'Runs', 'Result']} rows={actionsRows} />
      <p class="note">On the hosted test install <code>graft-m4a</code> and the separate runner account, 14 to 17 November 2026, with runner 2.338.2. Every gate row of Milestone 4 now passes; the <a href="/status/#m4">Status page</a> tracks what is still in build.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="agents-scale" aria-labelledby="agents-scale-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Agents at scale</p>
        <h2 id="agents-scale-h">Hundreds of agents on one repository, people still first</h2>
        <p>The AM1 gate runs an agent fleet against a single repository on a hosted test install, then checks that people's commits and merges stay fast and the leader never changes.</p>
      </div>
      <Table head={['Check', 'Runs', 'Result']} rows={agentRows} />
      <p class="note">On hosted test installs, 18 to 24 November 2026. A row with three runs is judged on its median run. B0-32, B0-33 and B0-34 are met on the lead's rulings, which the operator accepted by closing AM1 on 24 November.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="release" aria-labelledby="release-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Release control</p>
        <h2 id="release-h">A bad release always has a way back</h2>
        <p>New versions roll out one site a day under releases the customer signs. Each check breaks a rollout on purpose, then reads back where every site ended up.</p>
      </div>
      <Table head={['Check', 'Runs', 'Result']} rows={releaseRows} />
      <p class="note">On the hosted test installs <code>graft-m4a</code> and <code>graft-m3f</code>, 22 to 24 November 2026. The vault ran on the runner account as a stand-in. Every clause of Milestone 5's gate now passes.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="identity aria-labelledby="identity-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Tokens and sign-in</p>
        <h2 id="identity-h">A revoked token stops working on the next write</h2>
        <p>Personal access tokens, GitHub App installation tokens and <code>gh auth login</code> run against the hosted edge with stock clients. Revocation is checked where it matters: the very next push.</p>
      </div>
      <Table head={['Check', 'Runs', 'Result']} rows={identityRows} />
      <p class="note">App installation tokens are stateless: the edge verifies their signature, and a revoke is a committed entry the next write reads. The signing key was rotated on the hosted install, and a token naming the retired key is refused. An earlier 100-round run lost one App-token revoke before it reached the edge, so that token rightly kept working; the gate counts the later run, in which all 100 revokes answered.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="latency" aria-labelledby="latency-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Latency</p>
        <h2 id="latency-h">What two continents cost a write</h2>
        <p>A write waits for one round to the nearest site in another region. That is the price of losing nothing when a region goes down. Reads from the leader's region pay no such round.</p>
      </div>
      <Table head={['Measure', 'Median', 'Tail', 'Gate']} rows={latencyRows} />
      <p class="note">The production Rust host passes the 600 ms commit line at 100 commands/s in 6 of 6 runs, and at 300 to 330 commands/s in 16 of 16, with no command failed in either.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="open" aria-labelledby="open-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="span-5">
          <h2 id="open-h">Still open</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="prose span-7 start-6">
          <ul>
            <li>The retired SSH path through Cloudflare Tunnel held 99.59% to 99.89% of sessions through container kills, between the 99% and 99.9% lines. SSH now runs through Spectrum, where stock clone and push pass. Its day-long kill test has not run yet and is carried to Milestone 6.</li>
            <li>In Milestones 0 and 1, one planted bug of 85 went uncaught: a resumed leader sending entries before they were durable. Later rounds catch every plant, though some needed a new test after a first run missed one; each results file records which.</li>
          </ul>
